00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,000
We need a wave of change.

2
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:06,000
A Plastic Ocean is a documentary film directed by Craig Leeson.

3
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:06,000
A documentary focused on plastic pollution in the world's oceans.

4
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:06,000
Himself, played by Craig Leeson.

5
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:06,000
Herself, played by Tanya Streeter.

6
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:06,000
The Ocean Cleanup is a nonprofit environmental engineering organization based in the Netherlands that develops and deploys
technology to extract plastic pollution from the oceans and to capture it in rivers before it can reach the ocean.

7
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:06,000
Their initial focus was on the Pacific Ocean and its garbage patch, and extended to
rivers in countries including Indonesia, Guatemala, and the United States.

8
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:06,000
The Ocean Cleanup was founded in 2013 by Boyan Slat, a
Dutch inventor who serves as its CEO.

9
00:54:00,000 --> 00:54:06,000
It develops both ocean and river based catch systems.

10
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
Its ocean system consists of a funnel shaped floating
barrier which is towed by two ships.

11
01:06:00,000 --> 01:06:06,000
The ocean system is deployed in oceanic gyres to collect marine debris.

12
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:06,000
The project aims to launch 10 or more approximately 2 km-long (1.2 mi) systems which they predict could
remove 50% of the debris in the Great Pacific Garbage Patch five years from deployment.

13
01:18:00,000 --> 01:18:06,000
The river system consists of a variety of floating barriers and extraction
systems which are anchored within rivers or at rivermouths.

14
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
The Ocean Cleanup also publishes scientific papers, and estimates that "1% of worlds rivers
(~1,000 rivers) are responsible for 80% of the pollution in the world's seas".

15
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:06,000
They aim to deploy their river systems in these 1,000 rivers.

16
01:36:00,000 --> 01:36:06,000
As of January 2026, the organization has removed over 50 million kilograms (50,000
metric tons)
of trash from rivers and the Great Pacific Garbage Patch.
